<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VisualMaterial> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#promptExtractionText> "Extract Visual Material elements from the image. Visual Material includes: people, objects, characters, scenes, graphics, and any salient visual elements. For each visual element found, provide: 1) A descriptive instance_name (e.g., 'smiling_girl', 'burning_house'), 2) A natural language label (e.g., 'the smiling girl', 'the burning house'), 3) A brief description of what you see. Focus on clearly visible, distinct visual elements that are important to understanding the meme. Example output format: {\"instance_name\": \"smiling_girl\", \"label\": \"the smiling girl\", \"description\": \"A young girl with a mischievous smile in the foreground\"}"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dramaticallyIronicalTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#outlinedStrokedOn> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "outlined stroked on"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#MotionBlurIntegrated> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Text following movement paths and kinetic lines within dynamic visuals in memes"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#consilientWith> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#relationIndex> "7"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TemporalRelation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Temporal Relation"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#verballyIronicalTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Indicates text saying the opposite of another dimension in memes, e.g. contradicting visual reality"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#entityOccluding> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#relationIndex> "15"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Scene> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Scene"@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01243#mapping_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The top text 'when your human says \"who's a good girl?\"' sets up a question directed at the dog, implying the dog is the subject of the praise. The visual material of the dog, with its happy, panting, self-satisfied expression, is mapped onto the textual role of 'good girl'. The dog's joyful demeanor is interpreted as the visual embodiment of the 'good girl' being praised."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#speechThoughtIntegratedWith> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "speech thought integrated with"@en .
