<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01243#semiotic_projection_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The user is projected onto the dog, which is visually depicted as the 'good girl' being praised. The text 'and you already know it's you' explicitly directs the user to identify with the dog's self-aware, joyful response to being called 'good girl'. The dog's happy expression and direct gaze create a humorous, anthropomorphic interaction that invites the user to imagine themselves in the dog's place, thus projecting the user's identity onto the dog as the subject of the praise."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#temporallyRelatedTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#shadowGlowEffectOn> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#semiTransparentOverlaidOn> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#relationIndex> "36"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#superimposedOn> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#relationIndex> "40"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01243#dog_praise_culture> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme targets individuals who are familiar with the common cultural practice of pet owners praising their dogs with phrases like 'good girl' or 'good boy' as a form of positive reinforcement. This cultural reference is central to the humor and relatability of the meme, making it most effective for audiences who have experienced or observed this interaction in human-animal relationships, particularly in Western cultures where dogs are often anthropomorphized and treated as family members."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VerbalIrony> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Verbal Irony"@en .
